# Byte-exact test fixtures.
#
# hooks/_test_fixtures/unicode-hygiene/ holds files whose entire purpose is to
# carry one specific codepoint at one specific offset. test_unicode_hygiene.py
# asserts those offsets literally, and one test asserts that the file's SHA-256
# is unchanged by the scan-and-sanitize flow.
#
# Git's default text handling breaks both. With core.autocrlf=true, which is
# the Windows default and is set on the machine these were authored on, a fresh
# clone rewrites every LF to CRLF: measured on class-zero-width.md, 54 bytes
# become 60 and every asserted offset after the first newline shifts by up to
# six. The fixtures would still look correct in an editor while the suite went
# red on any Windows checkout, and a SHA committed in a wiki page's source
# field would no longer match the file it names.
#
# eol=lf rather than -text, because none of these files contains a CR byte, so
# pinning the line ending is sufficient and they stay readable as text diffs.
hooks/_test_fixtures/unicode-hygiene/* text eol=lf
